    kindTel: 'Phone', kindMail: 'Email',
    goesTo: 'The browser will go to', copy: 'Copy', copied: 'Copied!', open: 'Open the link',
    ssid: 'Network name', pass: 'Password', prot: 'Security', hidden: 'Hidden', yes: 'yes',
    name: 'Name', org: 'Organisation', job: 'Job title', phone: 'Phone', mail: 'Email', site: 'Website',
    warnAt: '<b>Careful: this address is deceptive.</b> Everything before the “@” is not the site. The browser will go to',
    warnPuny: '<b>Careful: the host name is written with special characters.</b> Such names are made to look like well-known ones — the letters look identical but the site is different. The real name:',
    warnScheme: '<b>Careful: unusual link scheme.</b> We do not offer to follow a link like this:',
    noOpen: 'Only ordinary http and https links can be opened.',
    n1: '<b>The scanner never opens anything by itself.</b> What it reads is shown as text, and the decision to follow it is yours. That is not caution for its own sake: a QR code cannot be read by eye, and whole families of scams are built on it — somebody else’s sticker pasted over the real one on a parking meter, in a café, on a bill.',
    n2: '<b>We name where the link actually leads.</b> In <code>https://bank.example@evil.example</code> the browser goes to <code>evil.example</code>: everything before the “@” is not the site. We flag such an address and show the real host on its own line.',
    n3: '<b>Look-alike names are flagged too.</b> Some host names are written with characters visually identical to Latin ones: “аpple.com” with a Cyrillic “а” looks genuine. Browsers show such names in the technical <code>xn--…</code> form, and we warn about it.',
    n4: '<b>The Wi-Fi password is shown correctly.</b> In the network string special characters are escaped with a backslash; a scanner that does not remove it shows the password with stray slashes. We remove it, so the password can simply be copied.',
